# Local development Compose credentials.
#
# Copy to `.env` IN THIS DIRECTORY (`v3/infra/docker/.env`) and fill every blank
# before running `docker compose -f v3/infra/docker/docker-compose.yml up -d`.
# Compose resolves its environment file relative to the compose file, so a
# `.env` at the repository root will NOT be read for these values.
#
# `.env` is ignored by `v3/.gitignore`. Never commit it, and never paste these
# values into a shell that keeps history or into a command line, where they are
# visible in process listings.
#
# Every entry below is REQUIRED. `docker-compose.yml` declares them as
# `${VAR:?...}`, so Compose refuses to start with a clear message rather than
# falling back to a default. That is deliberate: a default password in a
# committed file is a credential, and one that silently keeps working after a
# rotation is worse than none at all.
#
# Generate each value independently -- never reuse one across two variables, and
# never reuse a local value in CI or production:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# These are DEVELOPMENT credentials for containers on your own machine. They are
# not a deployment topology and must never be copied into a real environment.

# PostgreSQL superuser password for the local `bc-v3-pg` container.
# Note: PostgreSQL applies this only when initialising an EMPTY data volume. On
# an already-initialised cluster, change the password with `ALTER ROLE` instead
# and keep this value in sync for the next clean start.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# MinIO root credentials for the local `bc-v3-minio` container.
# MINIO_ROOT_USER is the S3 access key id; MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD is the secret.
# Both are required, and both must be set before first start.
MINIO_ROOT_USER=
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=
